being precisely when stdout may hold a partially written secret. RECOVERY COMMANDS --json (gitlab#146, github#64): list-recovery, recover, add-recovery and remove-recovery gain a --json flag emitting a single JSON document on stdout instead of the human report (which otherwise still goes to stderr); list-recovery --json reports each slot's id, type and full key_id (the human view truncates it to 16 chars; the value is bounded since it comes from an untrusted file header) and add-recovery --json reports the output path, slot type and which credential produced the slot, so a slot wrapped under a planted $OPENSSL_ENCRYPT_ADD_RECOVERY_PASSPHRASE is distinguishable from one the user typed; a generated recovery code is NEVER written to stdout or stderr in JSON mode — it unwraps the file's key and neither stream is safe for it (stdout is the usual target of shell redirection and is merged by 2>&1, stderr reaches scrollback and log files) — so --recovery-code-out PATH has the tool write it to a file it creates 0600 and refuses to overwrite, with only the path in the JSON, --add-code --json without a destination is refused rather than silently withholding the credential, and the code is written before the envelope is modified so the credential cannot be lost to a failed write — if the slot write then fails the code file is left in place and reported rather than deleted, since a failure does not prove the slot was not written and an orphan code opens nothing; prerequisite for the desktop GUI Recovery Slots screen. GLOBAL FLAGS AFTER A SUBCOMMAND (gitlab#171, github#89): --debug, --verbose, --quiet and the other top-level flags are declared on the main parser, so argparse rejects them once a subcommand has been seen, and preprocess_global_args (which exists to relocate them to the front) kept its own hand-maintained copy of the subcommand list that had drifted to 20 of the 42 real subcommands - for the missing 22, including identity, keyserver, telemetry, plugin, hsm, test, sign, verify-signature and the recovery commands, argv was returned unchanged and the subparser rejected the flag with exit 2, so with the desktop GUI's debug toggle on every one of its 19 affected call sites failed before doing any work; both the preprocessor and main() now read one SUBPARSER_COMMANDS constant so the two cannot drift again, five subparsers that declare their own --quiet use default=argparse.SUPPRESS so the relocated value is not silently overwritten when argparse copies the subcommand namespace back over the parent's, and tests assert the flag survives a full parse rather than merely that it was moved. KEYSERVER LOGIN CREDENTIAL REDACTION (gitlab#171, github#89): keyserver login takes the client_id as a POSITIONAL argument, which SECRET_VALUE_CLI_OPTIONS cannot match because it keys on option names, and the login body is {client_id} with the password optional so the client_id alone yields access and refresh tokens - sanitize_argv_for_debug now redacts the value after any subcommand in the new SECRET_POSITIONAL_SUBCOMMANDS set (set-token, login) and three keyserver plugin logger.info calls no longer interpolate it; newly reachable rather than newly introduced, and only on this development branch, since argparse previously rejected --debug after keyserver so neither path could run in any released version; third-party HTTP loggers (urllib3, requests, httpx, httpcore, asyncio) are additionally clamped to WARNING under --debug, because --debug sets the ROOT logger to DEBUG and urllib3 logs the full request line while the keyserver interpolates a fingerprint or email address into the request path, which would have written contact metadata to stderr and the GUI's persistent debug log outside the debug_secret() chokepoint. SHARED CREDENTIAL ENVIRONMENT CHANNEL (gitlab#154 github#72, gitlab#159 github#77): the keyed-hidden-mode second password and the sign signer-identity passphrase could be supplied only through a getpass() prompt, which reads /dev/tty, so neither the desktop GUI nor any CI caller could drive them at all - blocking GUI hidden-header support and the Sign screen; OPENSSL_ENCRYPT_SECOND_PASSWORD and OPENSSL_ENCRYPT_SIGNER_PASSPHRASE now carry them, and rather than a third and fourth private copy of the mechanism the read-once-and-delete channel built for recovery slots (gitlab#144) is generalized into modules/credential_env.py and shared: each variable is read once and removed from the environment immediately, including when a higher-priority source supersedes it, so it is not inherited by a later child process (several call sites run subprocess.run() with no env=); it is registered for audit-log redaction BEFORE the deletion, since in between it would match neither the live-environment check nor the fingerprint registry; a blank value is refused rather than falling through to a prompt a GUI subprocess could never answer; and the value is validated without being modified, so a credential set through one channel still opens through another; neither variable can ENABLE the feature it belongs to, which matters most for the second password since a non-None value there is what switches keyed hidden mode on - a bare exported variable would otherwise write every file with a keyed hidden header the user never chose and cannot reproduce, readable by whoever planted it and locking the user out of their own metadata, so --hidden-header (or an explicit --second-password form) must request the credential before the variable is read, and an HSM-only signer identity is never given a passphrase merely because a variable is set; on the new environment channel a value containing a newline is refused rather than silently trimmed, because the file-descriptor and prompt channels both stop at the first newline so a trailing newline (exactly what passing a GUI text field's contents produces) would derive a different key from the same passphrase typed at the prompt with no error at any point, while the pre-existing --second-pass