leaked through three fields (signer_fingerprint printed on the unknown-signer error path before any cryptographic check, algorithm echoed by the unsupported-algorithm error pre-verification, and the display-only component name - deliberately excluded from the signed payload, so any tamperer can rewrite it on a VALID signature - printed inside the GOOD-signature verdict block), and a keyserver's raw HTTP error body (unbounded remote text) reached the terminal through exception messages; the fix validates at every boundary and sanitizes at every display: import_public, Identity.generate, Identity.load and PublicKeyBundle now validate email (string, at most 320 chars, no control characters), fingerprint (the colon-separated lowercase hex shape the tool has always written) and created_at (at most 64 chars, no controls), parse_signature format-validates signer_fingerprint, algorithm and every component name, keyserver HTTP error bodies are truncated and sanitized, identity.json is read bounded/UTF-8/through the JSON security validator so a hostile store directory cannot DoS identity list, and a display sanitizer escapes C0 controls, DEL, the C1 range (one-byte CSI introducers included), backslash (so escaped output is unambiguous) and the bidi/format controls (honoured by VTE/Kitty, able to visually reverse an email within its line) at every terminal display of identity name/email/fingerprint, the keyserver trust prompt and search display, the TOFU key-change warning, the verified-signature sender line and every identity CLI error path - escaping rather than stripping keeps the evidence visible, and contacts and cached keyserver bundles imported by earlier versions with crafted fields are neutralized at display time by the same sanitizer. GUI IDENTITY LISTING (gitlab#183, github#100): the desktop GUI ran 'identity list --include-contacts --json', but identity list declared no --json flag, so argparse exited 2 and the GUI swallowed the failure into empty lists - the Identity Management screen, the Decrypt tab's identity selector and the main window all showed an empty identity list with no error, so GUI identity listing had never worked (third emitted-but-nonexistent flag after gitlab#164 and gitlab#181); identity list --json now emits a single JSON document on stdout (own/contacts lists of name, email, fingerprint, kem_algorithm, sig_algorithm, created_at) and nothing else, since the GUI feeds all of stdout to a JSON parser, with the human report unchanged without the flag; the JSON channel is deliberately NOT display-sanitized (machine-readable, the transport escapes control characters, the consumer renders), so display safety belongs to the renderer - the GUI sanitizes at the decode boundary rather than per widget (a per-widget pass had already missed the recipient picker and the signature-verification picker, the two controls deciding who can read the plaintext and whose signature is trusted), its sanitizer now escapes rather than blanks (matching the CLI's escape-not-strip rule) and covers the C1 range, the bidi/format controls, U+2028/U+2029 and the zero-width set while leaving ZWNJ/ZWJ untouched as load-bearing in Persian/Indic scripts and emoji sequences, and since Flutter honours bidi overrides and treats U+2028/U+2029 as mandatory line breaks those characters are now rejected at the CLI import boundary too, alongside gitlab#172's terminal-control class; two failure modes that let this bug hide are closed with it - the GUI no longer turns a failed identity list into an empty identity list (it raises, so the screen's previously-dead error banner renders) and a store entry that fails to load is reported rather than silently absent in both the JSON (skipped key) and the human output, since presenting a short list as complete would silently drop a recipient or make an own identity look deleted. IDENTITY NAMESPACE COLLISION (gitlab#173, GHSA-8gmx-w9m8-vx7q): get_by_name resolves own identities before contacts, but add_identity chose its destination purely from whether the identity was an own one, so importing a contact whose name matched an existing own identity created a shadowed contact entry - invisible while the own identity existed, since every lookup resolved the own identity first - and delete_identity removed only the first location it found, so deleting the own identity left the shadow behind and get_by_name then resolved to the contact's keys under a name the user trusts; recipient resolution goes through get_by_name, making this a live key substitution where files encrypted to that name go to the attacker's key, and the TOFU key-change dialogue is no defence because it gates a CHANGED KEY (passable with --allow-key-change, and silent when the fingerprints match); add_identity now refuses a name that already exists as the other kind in both directions and independently of the key-change gate, delete_identity removes both locations so a pre-existing shadow cannot be promoted, and IdentityStore.find_shadowed_names() plus identity list (human output and the --json shadowed key) surface a collision that is otherwise invisible. Review of the fix caught that deleting both sides had become a trap - the warning told users to remove the entry they did not intend to keep, but identity delete had no way to do that and removed both, destroying the own identity's private keys (making every file encrypted to it unreadable) and the contact's TOFU pin, so a later import of that name would be accepted as first use with no key-change warning; identity delete now takes --kind own|contact|both (default both), shows both entries and both fingerprints before the confirmation and states what each deletion costs, and 'contacts' is reserved as an entry name because it is the store's own container directory, so an entry of that name was written INTO it - unlistable yet resolvable, and deleting it would have removed every pinned contact in the store. ENVELOPE REWRITE DATA LOSS (gitlab#148): _write_envelope